    "questionText": "A 27-year-old woman reports eight months of progressively heavier menstrual bleeding along with fatigue, palpitations, and dyspnea after climbing one flight of stairs. Temperature is 37.0 degrees Celsius, heart rate 108 per minute, blood pressure 112 over 70 mmHg, and respiratory rate 18 per minute. She has conjunctival pallor, spoon-shaped nails, and a soft systolic flow murmur at the left sternal border. Hemoglobin is 6.2 g per dL with an MCV of 68 fL and a ferritin of 4 ng per mL. Room air arterial blood gas shows pH 7.42, PaO2 97 mmHg, PaCO2 36 mmHg, and an arterial oxygen saturation of 98 percent, and bedside pulse oximetry reads 98 percent. Which of the following best explains her exertional symptoms?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Arterial PO2 is reduced because of impaired alveolar gas exchange",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Impaired gas exchange lowers PaO2 and widens the alveolar to arterial gradient, but her PaO2 is 97 mmHg on room air with a normal PaCO2 of 36 mmHg and no hypoxemia whatsoever. Iron deficiency from menorrhagia does not injure the alveolar capillary membrane. This would be the correct answer if she had pneumonia, pulmonary edema, or pulmonary embolism producing a genuinely low PaO2 with a widened gradient."
      },
      {
        "text": "Hemoglobin saturation is reduced by a rightward shift of the curve",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. A rightward shift alters the affinity of the hemoglobin that is present rather than its quantity, and her measured saturation is 98 percent, which is not reduced at all. Chronic anemia does raise red cell 2,3-BPG and shift the curve rightward, but that is a helpful compensation that improves unloading rather than the cause of her symptoms. This would be the correct answer if the vignette showed a low saturation at a normal PaO2 together with an elevated P50 as the primary abnormality."
      },
      {
        "text": "Arterial oxygen content is low despite a normal PO2 and saturation",
        "explanation": "Correct. Arterial oxygen content equals about 1.34 times the hemoglobin concentration times the fractional saturation, plus a small dissolved term of 0.003 times the PaO2. Her PaO2 of 97 mmHg and saturation of 98 percent are normal because her lungs work well and the hemoglobin she does have is fully loaded, but with a hemoglobin of only 6.2 g per dL the carrying capacity itself is roughly 40 percent of normal, giving a content near 8 mL per dL instead of about 20. Because delivery is content multiplied by cardiac output, she compensates with a heart rate of 108 and a hyperdynamic flow murmur, and still decompensates with exertion. This is the classic pattern of anemic hypoxia, in which partial pressure and saturation are preserved while content and delivery are not."
      },
      {
        "text": "Dissolved plasma oxygen is reduced in proportion to the low hemoglobin",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Dissolved oxygen depends only on the PaO2 and the solubility coefficient of 0.003 mL per dL per mmHg, so at a PaO2 of 97 mmHg it is about 0.29 mL per dL regardless of how much hemoglobin is present. That trivial quantity is unchanged by her anemia and could never account for her fatigue and dyspnea. This would be the correct answer if she were breathing high inspired oxygen in a hyperbaric chamber, where dissolved oxygen becomes a clinically meaningful fraction of total content."
      },
      {
        "text": "Tissue oxygen extraction is blocked at the mitochondrial cytochromes",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Blockade of mitochondrial electron transport produces histotoxic hypoxia with an abnormally high venous oxygen saturation and profound lactic acidosis, and her pH of 7.42 and gradual eight month course fit neither. Her tissues are extracting oxygen normally, but too little oxygen is arriving because carrying capacity is low. This would be the correct answer if she had cyanide or hydrogen sulfide poisoning with abrupt collapse and arterialized venous blood."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 2,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: In anemia the PaO2 and the SaO2 are NORMAL while oxygen CONTENT and delivery are low; saturation reports what fraction of the hemoglobin present is loaded, never how much hemoglobin there is.",
