    "category": "Respiratory Physiology: Airway Resistance",
    "questionText": "A 24-year-old man with mild intermittent asthma, currently asymptomatic, volunteers for a respiratory mechanics study. A pressure-sensing catheter is advanced through a bronchoscope and lateral airway pressure is recorded at successive branching generations during quiet tidal breathing. The investigators find that the largest pressure drop per unit of airflow occurs in the medium-sized bronchi, approximately the fourth through eighth generations (subsegmental and smaller bronchi), whereas the summed pressure drop across all airways smaller than 2 mm in diameter accounts for less than 20 percent of total resistance. When the protocol is repeated at a higher lung volume, total resistance falls, and it falls further when the subject breathes a mixture of 80 percent helium and 20 percent oxygen. Which of the following best explains why the airways smaller than 2 mm contribute so little to total airway resistance?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Flow in the terminal bronchioles is turbulent rather than laminar",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Turbulence raises the pressure cost of flow, so if it truly occurred in the terminal bronchioles it would make the small airways a major resistive site, which is the opposite of what was measured. Because the enormous total cross-sectional area of the distal tree makes linear gas velocity extremely low there, the Reynolds number is small and flow in those airways is laminar or even diffusive. This is also why the helium-oxygen mixture helped: low-density gas reduces density-dependent turbulent and transitional flow, and it works precisely because that flow lives in the large and medium central airways. This would be the correct answer if the measurement site were the trachea or a main bronchus during heavy exercise, where high linear velocity and a wide lumen drive the Reynolds number above the turbulent threshold."
      },
      {
        "text": "The small airways in parallel provide an enormous cross-sectional area",
        "explanation": "Correct. Each individual bronchiole is narrow and by the Poiseuille relationship has a high resistance of its own, since resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of the radius. However, dichotomous branching produces tens of thousands of these airways arranged in parallel (about 65,000 terminal bronchioles alone), and resistances in parallel add as reciprocals, so the total conductance of the distal generations is huge and their combined resistance is tiny. The medium-sized bronchi of roughly the fourth through eighth generations are far fewer in number and are the point at which total cross-sectional area is still small while flow velocity is still high, which is exactly where the catheter recorded the largest pressure drop. The fall in resistance at higher lung volume reflects radial traction from surrounding alveolar septa widening intraparenchymal airways, and the additional fall on heliox reflects reduced density-dependent flow in the central airways, both consistent with a central, not peripheral, resistive site. Clinically this is why the small airways are called the silent zone: substantial disease can accumulate there before total resistance or FEV1 changes appreciably."
      },
      {
        "text": "Each individual small airway has a wider lumen than a lobar bronchus",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. This reverses the anatomy, since an individual bronchiole measures well under 2 mm while a lobar bronchus is roughly 10 mm across, and the study explicitly defined the peripheral airways by their small diameter. The low resistance of the distal tree comes from summation of many airways in parallel, not from any single airway being wide. This would be the correct answer if the comparison were between one markedly dilated bronchiectatic distal airway and a lobar bronchus narrowed to a slit by an endobronchial tumor, where the individual caliber relationship would indeed be inverted."
      },
      {
        "text": "Radial traction from alveolar septa is absent in the medium bronchi",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Radial traction is a real and important phenomenon, but it explains why resistance changes with lung volume, not why the site of maximal resistance is central; in this subject it is the reason resistance fell when the maneuver was repeated at higher lung volume. The medium bronchi are relatively protected from collapse because they carry cartilaginous plates, so lack of tethering is not what makes them the dominant resistive segment. This would be the correct answer if the question asked why total airway resistance rises steeply as lung volume approaches residual volume, since loss of alveolar tethering narrows the intraparenchymal small airways at low volumes."
      },
      {
        "text": "Cartilage in the small airways holds them open during quiet breathing",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Cartilage disappears from the airway wall at the level of the bronchioles, so airways under 2 mm have no cartilaginous support at all and depend instead on the radial traction of surrounding parenchyma. Their low contribution to resistance is a matter of parallel arrangement, not structural rigidity. This would be the correct answer if the airway under discussion were a lobar or segmental bronchus, where cartilaginous plates do keep the lumen patent and prevent collapse during a forced expiratory effort."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: The MEDIUM-sized bronchi of roughly the FOURTH through EIGHTH generations are the site of greatest airway resistance, because the small airways sit in PARALLEL and their vast combined cross-sectional area makes their summed resistance trivial; resistance falls as lung volume rises (radial traction) and falls with low-density heliox (less turbulent central flow).",
    "lectureSource": "CV51: Lung Mechanics"
