    "category": "Respiratory Physiology: Shunt Versus V/Q Mismatch",
    "questionText": "A 61-year-old man is admitted with fever, cough, and progressive dyspnea. Temperature is 38.9 degrees C, respiratory rate 30 per minute, and oxygen saturation 84 percent on room air. Chest radiograph shows dense consolidation of the entire right lower lobe. He is placed on a tight-fitting mask delivering 100 percent oxygen for 20 minutes, after which arterial blood gas shows a partial pressure of oxygen of only 61 mm Hg. A second patient on the same ward with a chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation had a room air oxygen tension of 55 mm Hg that rose to 340 mm Hg on the same 100 percent oxygen. Which of the following best explains why the first patient failed to respond to supplemental oxygen while the second responded briskly?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Blood traversing the consolidated lobe never contacts ventilated alveoli and cannot be oxygenated",
        "explanation": "Correct. The consolidated right lower lobe is filled with inflammatory exudate, making those alveoli completely unventilated while pulmonary arterial blood continues to flow through them, which is the definition of a true right-to-left intrapulmonary shunt with a V/Q of zero. Because that blood never contacts alveolar gas, raising the inspired oxygen fraction cannot enrich it, and it returns to the left atrium with mixed venous oxygen content regardless of what the mask delivers. The small rise seen with oxygen comes only from the modest extra dissolved oxygen added to the well ventilated lung units, and because the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve is flat at high tensions this cannot compensate for admixture of desaturated shunt blood. In the second patient the abnormal units are poorly ventilated rather than unventilated, so a high inspired oxygen fraction eventually raises alveolar oxygen even in slowly filling units and corrects the hypoxemia. This differential response is the classic bedside method for separating shunt from V/Q mismatch, and a shunt fraction above roughly 30 percent makes oxygen therapy alone essentially futile."
      },
      {
        "text": "The consolidated lobe has a high V/Q ratio that wastes ventilation and limits oxygen loading",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. A high V/Q region is ventilated but underperfused, which describes dead space rather than the airless consolidated lobe seen on this patient radiograph, and dead space raises carbon dioxide far more than it lowers oxygen. Dead space units also respond readily to supplemental oxygen because by definition they are receiving fresh gas, so this mechanism cannot explain his refractory hypoxemia. This would be the correct answer if the question had asked why his dead space fraction was elevated, as in acute pulmonary embolism with a clear chest radiograph."
      },
      {
        "text": "Alveolar hypoventilation from respiratory muscle fatigue is limiting his alveolar oxygen tension",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. His respiratory rate of 30 per minute indicates he is hyperventilating rather than hypoventilating, and pure hypoventilation is the one cause of hypoxemia that corrects almost immediately with even modest supplemental oxygen. Hypoventilation would also raise his arterial carbon dioxide and leave his alveolar-arterial gradient normal, neither of which fits a patient with dense unilateral consolidation. This would be the correct answer if the question had asked the mechanism of hypoxemia in an obtunded patient with opioid overdose, a slow shallow respiratory pattern, a rising carbon dioxide, and a clear chest film."
      },
      {
        "text": "Thickening of the alveolar capillary membrane is producing a diffusion limitation to oxygen transfer",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Diffusion limitation requires blood to be exposed to alveolar gas across an abnormal barrier, and in a lobe packed with exudate there is no gas exposure at all, so the problem is absent ventilation rather than slow transfer. Diffusion limited hypoxemia also improves substantially with supplemental oxygen because the higher alveolar tension steepens the driving gradient across the thickened membrane. This would be the correct answer if the question had asked the mechanism of hypoxemia in chronic interstitial lung disease with desaturation appearing mainly during exercise."
      },
      {
        "text": "Loss of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction has diverted blood flow away from his normal lung units",
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts flow away from poorly oxygenated regions rather than away from healthy ones, and in this patient it is actually the compensatory mechanism partially limiting his shunt fraction. If this reflex failed entirely his hypoxemia would be worse, but its presence or absence does not change the fundamental fact that unventilated alveoli cannot be reached by inspired oxygen. This would be the correct answer if he had been given a potent nonselective pulmonary vasodilator such as a nitroprusside infusion, which blunts the reflex and acutely worsens oxygenation."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: Hypoxemia from V/Q mismatch CORRECTS with 100 percent oxygen, while hypoxemia from a true shunt does NOT, because shunted blood never contacts ventilated alveoli.",
    "lectureSource": "CV52: Ventilation-Perfusion Relationships"
